    <p class="legend"><span><span class="sw sw-addr" aria-hidden="true"></span> the machine's, shared</span><span><span class="sw sw-num" aria-hidden="true"></span> one universe's own</span><span><span class="sw sw-off" aria-hidden="true"></span> generated, transient</span></p>
  <Fragment slot="caption">A universe is cheap because the expensive things are in the top band. What collides when you run a stack twice is in the middle one.</Fragment>
</Figure>
</section>

<section id="lasts">
  <h2 id="lasts"><a href="#lasts">What survives what</a></h2>
  <p><code>down</code> takes the containers away and leaves everything you would mind losing. <code>rm</code> is the one destructive command, which is why it asks for the universe's name typed back.</p>
  <div class="tw"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Thing</th><th scope="col">Where it lives</th><th scope="col">After <code>down</code></th><th scope="col">After <code>rm</code></th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">Branches and commits</td><td data-l="Lives">The clone</td><td data-l="down"><span class="m y">kept</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m y">kept</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">The clone and its git objects</td><td data-l="Lives"><code>&lt;root&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/</code></td><td data-l="down"><span class="m y">kept</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m y">kept</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">Images and the build cache</td><td data-l="Lives">The daemon, not namespaced</td><td data-l="down"><span class="m y">kept</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m y">kept</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">The shared proxy</td><td data-l="Lives">Its own compose project</td><td data-l="down"><span class="m y">kept</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m y">kept</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">Worktrees, with your uncommitted edits</td><td data-l="Lives"><code>&lt;root&gt;/universes/&lt;universe&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/</code></td><td data-l="down"><span class="m y">kept</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m n">gone</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">Volumes, with your data</td><td data-l="Lives">The daemon, named per app per universe</td><td data-l="down"><span class="m y">kept</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m n">gone</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">The state file, with its hostnames and chosen ports</td><td data-l="Lives"><code>&lt;root&gt;/universes/&lt;universe&gt;/universe.yaml</code></td><td data-l="down"><span class="m y">kept</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m n">gone</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">Containers</td><td data-l="Lives">The daemon, one compose project per app</td><td data-l="down"><span class="m n">removed</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m n">gone</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">App networks</td><td data-l="Lives">The daemon, made by compose</td><td data-l="down"><span class="m n">removed</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m n">gone</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Thing">The generated fragments</td><td data-l="Lives">Beside the checkouts</td><td data-l="down"><span class="m p">left, rewritten next start</span></td><td data-l="rm"><span class="m n">gone</span></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>So <code>down</code> then <code>up</code> puts you back where you were, data included. <a href="lifecycle.html">Life of a universe</a> walks the same ground command by command, and <a href="../concepts/storage.html">Storage and data</a> is the long version.</p>
</section>

<section id="net">
  <h2 id="net"><a href="#net">Networking, in one picture</a></h2>
  <p>No universe publishes a host port — two copies of one stack cannot both have port 8000, and the first collision would make all the other namespacing pointless. Instead one proxy holds one set of ports for the whole machine and tells the universes apart by the hostname in the request.</p>
  <Figure>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 628" role="img" aria-labelledby="nt nd">
      <title id="nt">The path of a request from a browser through the shared proxy into one app's own network</title>
      <desc id="nd">A browser sends api-u3.example.test to the one shared proxy. The proxy is connected to the private network of each app: universe 3's api app on acme-u3-api_default, holding the services web and db, and universe 4's api app on acme-u4-api_default, holding its own web and db. The request lands in universe 3. No network is shared between the two, so each db is its own.</desc>

      <rect class="d-box" x="170" y="20" width="380" height="56" rx="12"/>
      <text class="d-t" x="360" y="56" text-anchor="middle">a browser on any device</text>
      <path class="d-flow" d="M360 76V118"/>
      <path class="d-head" d="M360 136l-10-18h20z"/>
      <text class="d-ad" x="378" y="112">api-u3.example.test</text>

      <rect class="d-bar" x="20" y="146" width="680" height="86" rx="12"/>
      <text class="d-h" x="360" y="186" text-anchor="middle">the one shared proxy</text>
      <text class="d-m" x="360" y="216" text-anchor="middle">joins each app's own network</text>

      <path class="d-flow" d="M180 232V274M540 232V274"/>
      <path class="d-head" d="M180 292l-10-18h20zM540 292l-10-18h20z"/>

      <rect class="d-box d-on" x="20" y="292" width="320" height="240" rx="12"/>
      <text class="d-a" x="180" y="336" text-anchor="middle">universe 3</text>
      <text class="d-m" x="180" y="366" text-anchor="middle">app api</text>
      <text class="d-m" x="180" y="398" text-anchor="middle">acme-u3-api_default</text>
      <path class="d-sep" d="M40 416H320"/>
      <rect class="d-box" x="35" y="432" width="130" height="46" rx="8"/>
      <text class="d-t" x="100" y="462" text-anchor="middle">web</text>
      <rect class="d-box" x="195" y="432" width="130" height="46" rx="8"/>
      <text class="d-t" x="260" y="462" text-anchor="middle">db</text>
      <text class="d-m" x="180" y="508" text-anchor="middle">plain names work here</text>

      <rect class="d-box" x="380" y="292" width="320" height="240" rx="12"/>
      <text class="d-a" x="540" y="336" text-anchor="middle">universe 4</text>
      <text class="d-m" x="540" y="366" text-anchor="middle">app api</text>
      <text class="d-m" x="540" y="398" text-anchor="middle">acme-u4-api_default</text>
      <path class="d-sep" d="M400 416H680"/>
      <rect class="d-box" x="395" y="432" width="130" height="46" rx="8"/>
      <text class="d-t" x="460" y="462" text-anchor="middle">web</text>
      <rect class="d-box" x="555" y="432" width="130" height="46" rx="8"/>
      <text class="d-t" x="620" y="462" text-anchor="middle">db</text>
      <text class="d-m" x="540" y="508" text-anchor="middle">plain names work here</text>

      <text class="d-m" x="360" y="574" text-anchor="middle">nothing is shared between them:</text>
      <text class="d-m" x="360" y="602" text-anchor="middle">universe 3's db is not universe 4's</text>
    </svg>
  <Fragment slot="caption">One set of ports for the machine, a private network per app, and a hostname that says which universe a request is for.</Fragment>
</Figure>
  <p>Inside one app, services reach each other by their compose service name — <code>db</code>, <code>redis</code>, <code>mailpit</code> — exactly as they would without multiversectl, because that network belongs to that app of that universe alone. That is why the names do not clash: universe 4's <code>db</code> is on a different network, and neither app can see it.</p>
  <p>Between apps of one universe, call them by their hostnames through the proxy. The whole story — entrypoints, domains, who can reach a universe, and serving https — is in <a href="../concepts/networking.html">Networking</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="data">
  <h2 id="data"><a href="#data">Data: whose is whose</a></h2>
  <p>Volumes are namespaced by compose project, so a database volume declared once in your compose file becomes a different volume in every universe, with no change to the file:</p>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="p">$ </span>docker volume ls | grep acme
acme-u3-api_postgres_data          <span class="c"># universe 3's database</span>
acme-u3-web_node_modules
acme-fix-login-api_postgres_data   <span class="c"># a different database entirely</span>
acme-fix-login-web_node_modules</pre></Terminal>
  <p>That is the main reason to run a second universe: a destructive migration on a review branch cannot reach what you were working on this morning. Images are the deliberate exception — they are expensive and usually identical, so universes share them and the layer cache.</p>
  <p>Your code works the same way: one clone per repository holds the history, and each universe gets a <code>git worktree</code> of it on its own branch. A large history is stored once however many universes exist, and one <code>fetch</code> updates every universe's view of the remote.</p>
  <p><a href="../concepts/storage.html">Storage and data</a> covers secrets, the generated files and exactly what <code>rm</code> destroys.</p>
</section>

<section id="runtime">
  <h2 id="runtime"><a href="#runtime">What is actually running</a></h2>
  <p>One container per service per app per universe, plus one proxy for the machine. Nothing else: multiversectl has no daemon or agent of its own, and nothing of it is running between commands.</p>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="p">$ </span>docker ps --format '&#123;&#123;.Names&#125;&#125;'
multiverse-proxy-traefik-1   <span class="c"># one for the machine</span>
acme-u3-api-web-1            <span class="c"># universe 3, app api, service web</span>
acme-u3-api-db-1
acme-u3-web-app-dev-1
acme-u4-api-web-1            <span class="c"># universe 4, the same services again</span>
acme-u4-api-db-1
acme-u4-web-app-dev-1</pre></Terminal>
  <p>The name is the whole hierarchy: multiverse, universe, app, service. It is what keeps two repositories that both call a service <code>app</code> from meeting, and it is how every command finds what belongs to what.</p>
  <p>The cost of a universe is therefore the cost of its containers' memory. That is the real limit on how many you run at once, and it is why <code>down</code> exists: a universe that is down costs disk, not memory, and comes back where it left off.</p>
</section>
</Page>
